# Every environment variable the API reads, with its default.
#
# Each line is commented out and shows the default the code uses; an empty
# value means there is no default. To change a setting when running with
# docker compose, copy this file to .env (git-ignored) and uncomment the lines
# you want: compose passes them to the API. compose.yaml always sets
# UPSTREAM_URL and FLAGS_URL itself, to wire the API to the mock and flagd
# containers. Outside compose, export the variables in your shell instead.
#
# A test (cmd/api/envexample_test.go) checks this file against the code and the
# README's Configuration table: same variables, same defaults.

# Base URL of the items upstream. Required, no default. May include a path
# prefix, but no query string or fragment.
# UPSTREAM_URL=

# Timeout for each upstream request.
# UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT=5s

# flagd OFREP base URL, e.g. http://flagd:8016. Unset disables flags.
# FLAGS_URL=

# Timeout for each flag evaluation.
# FLAGS_TIMEOUT=500ms

# How long a flag's value is kept after flagd serves it, and how often flagd
# is retried while it isn't answering. 0s asks flagd on every evaluation.
# FLAGS_CACHE_TTL=1s

# A flagd-format flag file whose defaults replace the embedded flagd/flags.json.
# FLAGS_FALLBACK_FILE=

# Listen address. Takes precedence over PORT.
# ADDR=:8080

# Listen port, used only when ADDR is unset.
# PORT=

# debug, info, warn or error.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# json or text.
# LOG_FORMAT=json

# How long in-flight requests get to finish on SIGTERM.
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=10s

# How long to keep serving after SIGTERM, reporting not ready on /readyz,
# before refusing new connections: time for a load balancer to stop sending
# requests. 0 stops accepting at once.
# SHUTDOWN_DELAY=0s

# API keys, comma-separated name:scope:sha256 entries, scope read or write.
# make api-key NAME=<who> SCOPE=read|write generates a key and its entry. Only
# the key's hash goes here. Required unless API_AUTH=off. (compose sets two
# local development keys when this is unset; docs/curl.md#api-keys.)
# API_KEYS=

# Instead of API_KEYS, a file of the same entries, one per line (# for
# comments), reloaded while the service runs, so keys can be added, removed or
# rotated without a restart. A file that won't parse changes nothing.
# API_KEYS_FILE=

# How often API_KEYS_FILE is checked for changes. A change applies once the
# file reads the same on two checks, so within twice this.
# API_KEYS_RELOAD=10s

# on, or off to let every request through without a key: for local
# development only; the service logs a warning when it's off.
# API_AUTH=on

# required, or open to let reads (GET) through without a key.
# API_AUTH_READS=required

# The most requests served at once; more get 503. 0 is no cap.
# MAX_IN_FLIGHT=512

# Requests per second each client (API key, or IP without one) may make on
# average, and in a burst; more get 429. RATE_LIMIT=0 is no limit. (compose
# sets RATE_LIMIT=0 when this is unset, so load tests measure the API.)
# RATE_LIMIT=100
# RATE_BURST=200

# The request-validation service every request is checked against, e.g.
# http://validator:8080. Unset checks nothing (and logs a warning).
# VALIDATOR_URL=

# Timeout for each validation call; it's on every request's path.
# VALIDATOR_TIMEOUT=300ms

# When the validation service can't answer: closed refuses requests (503),
# open lets them through unchecked.
# VALIDATOR_FAILURE_MODE=closed

# Log the client's IP address on each request's log lines: on or off. It's the
# connection's address (behind a load balancer, the balancer's), and personal
# data in many jurisdictions.
# LOG_CLIENT_IP=on

# How often to log the Go runtime's heap, memory and goroutines ("runtime
# stats"). 0s logs none.
# RUNTIME_STATS_INTERVAL=5m

# Where to serve the runtime profiles (pprof) and metrics (/metrics), e.g.
# 127.0.0.1:6061. Unset serves neither. Never on the API's port; keep it
# private: profiles expose internals.
# PPROF_ADDR=

# enforce rejects requests that break a header rule; report only logs them.
# HEADER_GUARD_MODE=enforce

# The most request headers allowed (1 to 1048576).
# HEADER_MAX_COUNT=100

# The largest any one request header may be, name and value, in bytes.
# HEADER_MAX_BYTES=8192

# The largest the request headers may be in total, in bytes.
# HEADER_MAX_TOTAL_BYTES=32768
